    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Diffuse Intrinsic Pontine Glioma (DIPG) is a brain tumor that originates in the pons of the brainstem. This devastating cancer most prominently affects children between the ages of 5 and 7 years of age, and comprises approximately 10-15% of all pediatric brain tumors. Recent studies have identified genomic alterations thought to drive development of DIPG, but the molecular effectors of tumorigenesis remain unclear.\r\nSingle cell qPCR experiments performed in the Monje Lab at Stanford have identified distinct subpopulations of cells from DIPG xenografts that exhibit distinct transcriptional profiles in contrast to other regions of the tumor. We hypothesized that this transcriptionally defined subpopulation of cells may act as cancer stem cells in DIPG. To further clarify the role of these subpopulations in DIPG tumor development, this project evaluated their potential to act as tumor-initiating stem cell populations.\r\nTo investigate whether this subpopulation could be acting as a tumor stem cell driving the initiation of DIPG, this population was screened for its ability to form neurospheres in vitro. Stem cell frequency estimates were calculated, and it was determined that cells seeded from the subpopulation of interest exhibited statistically equivalent stem cell frequencies when compared to cells from other regions of the same tumor.\r\nFurther, to evaluate whether the subpopulation of interest could act as a stem cell population contributing to the initiation of tumors, I monitored the expression profile maintenance of this population in culture over time. This analysis revealed that, over the course of several passages, the expression profile of this subpopulation is only weakly maintained.\r\nFinally, to further characterize the potential for this subpopulation to act as a tumor-initiating population, it was imperative to evaluate its proliferative capacity as compared to other regions of the same tumor. In the presence of growth factors, the subpopulation of interest proliferated at a higher rate than cells from other regions of the same tumor.\r\nWith the identification of a subpopulation that shows substantial growth capacity both in vitro and in vivo, I tested if this population is sensitive to pharmacological inhibition in hopes of identifying a viable therapeutic target for DIPG. Crenolanib, a PDGFR-specific RTK inhibitor, was screened for its ability to kill bulk DIPG cells. However, it appears that by itself, or in conjunction with another promising drug, Panobinostat, Crenolanib is ineffective at killing bulk DIPG cells.\r\nAltogether, this data suggets that the subpopulation of interest is not representative of a tumor-initiating stem cell population. However, it might actually be acting as a more differentiated transient amplifying population that doesn’t initiate the tumor, but instead contributes to its rapid growth.",
